  "id": "DrugBank_DB00741 - MONDO_17255",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Panuveitis (disease) through a mechanistic pathway involving Medrysone, a corticosteroid analog. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone is associated with Uveitis, which in turn is linked to Medrysone, and Medrysone is associated with Panuveitis. This indicates a potential pathway where corticosteroids may play a role in the development of uveitis, which can progress to Panuveitis. The presence of multiple indirect pathways, all involving Medrysone, supports the idea that corticosteroids may influence inflammatory processes in the eye, potentially contributing to the development of Panuveitis. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in modulating inflammatory responses, which is a well-documented mechanism in autoimmune and inflammatory diseases. The evidence is consistent with the known pharmacological effects of corticosteroids, which are used to suppress inflammation and immune responses.",
